# Where every on-Amiga harness runs, and what runs it.
#
# tools/check-harnesses.sh reads this and fails when a harness under tests/ or
# install/test/ is missing from it, when a row names a file that is gone, when
# a row claims a runner that does not invoke it, or when a row says `manual`
# for something a runner does invoke.  tools/ci.sh runs that check in the host
# stage, so it costs no emulator and no ROM.
#
# It exists because tests/tools/httpd-drill.py held twelve real WebDAV
# assertions that no script and no CI stage ever called, and nothing in the
# tree could tell that from a test that runs.  dist/make-dist.sh already
# refuses to pack a command that is built and listed nowhere; this is the same
# rule for harnesses.
#
# FORMAT
#
#   <path> : <runner>@<when> : <note>
#
# <runner> is one of
#
#   a file path      that file invokes it.  Checked: the path must appear in
#                    that file on a line that is not a comment.
#   chained:<path>   another harness invokes it.  That harness must itself
#                    have a row, and the chain must end at a runner.
#   manual           nothing invokes it.  <note> must say why, and the check
#                    fails if a runner does invoke it after all, so a row
#                    cannot go stale in the quiet direction.  A `manual` row
#                    takes no <when>: there is no schedule to state.
#
# <when> IS PART OF THE CLAIM, and it is computed and compared rather than
# believed.  Naming a runner says something invokes the harness; it does not
# say anything ever starts that runner.  Both of these had shipped here as one
# word:
#
#   fifteen rows named .github/workflows/emulator.yml, which is dispatch, a
#   nightly cron and tags, so nothing in it fires on a pull request; and
#   twelve rows named tools/ci.sh in a STAGE no workflow ever passed to it,
#   which is a person typing a command.
#
# Beside a row that runs on every push, all three read as covered.  So:
#
#   push      a workflow that fires on a branch push or a pull request
#   nightly   a workflow with a `schedule:`.  Tier 2 boots an emulator and
#             takes 7-20 minutes, so this is the right answer for it and it is
#             not a defect -- it is a different claim from `push`
#   release   a workflow that fires on a tag and nothing else
#   hand      no workflow runs it.  A person types it.  Legal, and the number
#             to watch: it is the one that looks like coverage from a distance
#
# tools/check-harnesses.sh resolves the harness through its runner -- and,
# for tools/ci.sh, through the stage function that holds it -- to the workflow
# triggers that actually fire, and fails on a row that declares anything else.
# A row cannot claim a schedule it does not have, and a harness whose runner
# starts firing on pushes cannot keep calling itself nightly.
#
# A `manual` row is a coverage hole with a reason, not a resolved question.
# The reason codes are:
#
#   peer      needs a second machine that can open a connection INTO the guest
#   bridged   needs the runner to be on a LAN it may bridge onto
#   bench     a measurement, not a pass/fail gate; there is nothing to go red
#   asset     needs something not ours to ship: a third-party client, a
#             licensed Workbench, another vendor's stack
#   windows   WinUAE, so a Windows host
#   UNWIRED   nothing prevents it running where the rest of tier 2 runs.
#             It simply has no home.  This is the list that should shrink.
#   SLIRP     written against SLIRP, and bridged is the only mode this project
#             uses.  It puts 10.0.2.x into the guest's own command list, its
#             staged configuration or its assertions, so it cannot be moved to
#             a real segment by passing -B: something has to discover the
#             gateway or the resolver at run time first.  A DIFFERENT fact
#             from UNWIRED -- the home exists, the harness cannot be put in it
#             as written -- and each note below says which literal is the
#             blocker.
#   RED       it runs where tier 2 runs, it asserts, and it is FAILING on
#             something that is not rig state.  Wiring it would put a known
#             red in a gate, so it stays out until the finding is answered --
#             but it is a different fact from UNWIRED and must not be counted
#             as one.  The note says what is red and on which check, so the
#             row is a defect report rather than a hole.

# RUNNING TWO OF THESE AT ONCE
#
# Safe by default, and it did not used to be.  Every harness below reaches the
# emulator through tools/amiberry-run.sh, which used to derive the guest's
# serial port by hashing its PER-ARM tag into 900 slots -- so two checkouts
# running the same arm always picked the same port, and with
# `serial_port=.../wait` the emulator blocks until something connects and
# nothing checked whose reader it was.  On 2026-08-25 three readers were
# measured on 12714 at once and the arms that read a foreign guest reported
# findings about a machine they had never booted: a transcript with two
# interfaces on one address, an arm hung at 185 s while its FASTER siblings
# passed in 16 s, two red rows in a release gate that were not defects.
#
# What replaced it is one lock directory, host-wide:
#
#     $AMINETXDUO_RIG_LOCKDIR, or ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/aminetxduo-rig-<uid>
#
# tools/emu-rig-lock.sh claims a serial port out of it by locking AND
# bind-probing, holds the reservation for the life of the run, verifies that
# the listener on that port is the emulator this run started, and has the guest
# echo a run token into the first line of its own transcript.  Nothing has to
# be set, nothing has to be remembered, and AMINETXDUO_RUN_TAG is for naming
# artefacts -- it never was the thing that kept two runs apart.
#
# THE TWO THAT STILL NEED SERIALIZING, and both refuse rather than corrupt:
#
#   a bridged ne2000_pcmcia guest.  Amiberry ignores mac= on that board and
#     hands every guest the HOST interface's address (gayle.cpp:1590,
#     tools/emu-board.sh emu_board_mac_honoured), so two of them on one LAN are
#     one hardware address at two IP addresses.  The second run stops with a
#     sentence saying to serialize.  -N a2065 honours mac= and is unaffected.
#   a harness that PINS a guest address with -a.  Left to itself
#     tests/perf/run-poolshare.sh claims one and pings it first; pin one and
#     you own the collision, including with the fifteen live machines a scan
#     found in 192.168.1.200-254.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- tier 1 --

# Nothing here yet: every harness below needs an emulator.

# --------------------------------------------------- invoked by a runner --

